tively heating their  homes and eating yeah what's the big  idea from the leader of the opposition  to try and ameliorate the biggest cost  of living crisis that we could have seen  for decades well the first thing is this  is not  um  only caused by the invasion of ukraine  this predates we were in a cost of  living crisis before russia invaded  ukraine so this has been something  that's been on the cards for a very long  time so what's the um what we've said is  do something now to deal with the energy  bills that people are facing as  everybody knows  most people are going to face an energy  bill rise of about 700 pounds in just a  few weeks time  and now with what's happened  internationally there's likely to be  another thousand pounds or so in the  autumn so we the labour party have said  take action what we would do  is have a windfall tax on the oil and  gas companies in the north sea who have  made more profit than they expected  because of the global price and you just  trust them to pass on them to consumers  more in their own words more money than  they know what to do with use that to  reduce the energy bills for households  by up to 600 pounds for those  particularly in need so a very direct  act that we would do  the government says no no no no we don't  want to ask any more of the oil and gas  companies we will ask working families  um and families to do it instead we'll  give them a loan  is there a worry they will give them a  loan of 200 which they will then pay  back over the next few years so the  political divide here is huge we say oil  and gas companies have to pay their part  particularly when they've had profits  they didn't expect the government says  we're going to hammer working families  but at the same time at the same time is  there not a concern firstly that this  windfall tax will not necessarily be  passed on to consumers but also that it  could almost act as a distance  to invest more in oil out of the north  sea at a time when we are facing big  questions about our energy security in  the uk these are windfall taxes these  are taxes on profits that those  companies did not expect to make the  global price has been higher than they  had anticipated therefore they've made  more profit than they anticipated they  have said they've used expressions such  as we've got more money than we know  what to do with the argument the  government has well we won't touch that  instead we'll ask families okay to foot  the bill by having a loan that they  don't want and paying it off is a it  just shows the complete difference in  priorities of the labour party that  wants to look after families who've got  energy bills they can't afford and the  government that's putting even more  pressure on those families boris johnson  reportedly going to saudi arabia next  year to talk about and getting oil  potentially moral potentially from there  there's obviously big human rights  concerns about saudi arabia are you  comfortable with us relying more on  saudi arabia for our energy if we're  trying to stop the use of russian oil if  i were the prime minister i would have a  uh  an energy strategy  here in the uk  i just want to get a clear answer well  there are human rights of course the  human rights issues  the direct question is should we be  accepting uh more oil from saudi arabia  we need an energy security strategy for  the uk i thought i'm really not clear  but i'm really i'm really just answering  me out and then i we need an uh an  energy strategy for the uk that's been  lacking for 10 years what i would like  to see is an energy strategy that's  based on the uk  not so reliant on other countries saudi  arabia or other countries one of the  things we've learned in the last few  years and particularly in the last few  weeks and months of over reliance  on international imports energy now  aren't we should we be looking to get  more energy from saudi arabia right now  what is it if there's anything not if  t